<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Skibbereen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Skibbereen is the largest town in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>, Ireland, on the River Ilen. It grew in the 17th century in a district long held by Gaelic families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odriscoll">O'Driscolls</a>, MacCarthys and O'Donovans. The town and its surroundings suffered some of the worst mortality of the Great Famine of 1845–1852, and its name became a byword for the disaster.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/skibbereen.jpg" alt="Skibbereen" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike Searle · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Bridge_Skibbereen_-_geograph.org.uk_-_498403.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Skibbereen, in Irish An Sciobairín, lies in West Cork on the N71 national secondary road. The River Ilen runs through the town and reaches the sea about 12 kilometres away at the village of Baltimore. In The Origin and History of Irish Names of Places (1869) the historian Patrick Weston Joyce suggested that the name comes from the small boats or skiffs, scibs, once common on this stretch of the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before 1600 most of the land around the town belonged to the MacCarthy Reagh dynasty. In 1631 Skibbereen received refugees fleeing the Sack of Baltimore, and its town charter dates from 1657. The Phoenix Society, a forerunner of the Fenian movement, was founded there in 1856, and the Fenian leader Jeremiah O'Donovan Rossa worked in the town. The Maid of Erin statue of 1904 stands on a memorial to four failed risings against British rule, those of 1798, 1803, 1848 and 1867. The town was a stop on the West Cork Railway, whose lines were built between 1851 and 1893 and closed by 1961.</p><p>During the Great Famine the district suffered heavily. The Skibbereen Heritage Centre estimates that 8,000 to 10,000 victims lie in the burial pits of Abbeystrewery cemetery near the town, and records, though of uncertain accuracy, show the population falling from 58,335 in 1841 to 32,412 in 1861. The local relief committee coordinated voluntary relief from the town and provided a model for soup kitchens and emigration schemes. The famine is the subject of the song Skibbereen, a conversation in which a son asks his father why he left the land he loved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 2,903 people in the town. The Skibbereen Heritage Centre holds a permanent exhibition on the famine, and the town was the focus of Ireland's first National Famine Memorial Day on 17 May 2009. The Skibbereen Eagle, founded in 1857, once told Lord Palmerston that it had its eye on him and on the Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; it was superseded by the Southern Star, founded in the town in 1889.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Skibbereen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their kingdom covered the coast and hinterland from around Clonakilty to Baltimore and inland toward <b>Skibbereen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/corcu-loigde">Corcu Loigde</a></li><li><blockquote>Donnubán's descendants, the O'Donovans, moved south into Cork, where they held lordships near <b>Skibbereen</b> for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/ui-fidgenti">Ui Fidgenti</a></li></ul></section>
